{"name":"Mineral Yellow","hex":"#d19a3b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mineral","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any naturally occurring solid material that has a (more or less) definite chemical composition and characteristic physical properties; especially, an inorganic and crystalline one.","Any inorganic material (as distinguished from animal or vegetable)."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["of, related to, or containing minerals"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mineral"},{"term":"yellow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of sunflower petals and lemons; the color obtained by mixing green and red light, or by subtracting blue from white light; the color evoked by light of wavelength around 580 nm; one of the three primary colors in subtractive color systems.","The middle light in a set of three traffic lights, the lighting of which indicates that drivers should stop short of the intersection if it is safe to do so."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a yellow hue.","Lacking courage."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/yellow"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mineral","blocks":[{"text":"In geology and mineralogy, a mineral or mineral species is, broadly speaking, a solid substance with a fairly well-defined chemical composition and a specific crystal structure that occurs naturally in pure form."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/S%C3%A9randite%2C_natrolite%2C_analcime%2C_aegirine_300-4-2112.JPG/330px-S%C3%A9randite%2C_natrolite%2C_analcime%2C_aegirine_300-4-2112.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mineral","wikidata":"Q7946"},{"title":"Yellow","blocks":[{"text":"Yellow is a color between green and orange on the spectrum of light. It is evoked by light with a dominant wavelength of roughly 575–585 nm. It is a primary color in subtractive color systems, used in painting or color printing. In the RGB color model, used to create colors on television and computer screens, yellow is a secondary color made by combining red and green at equal intensity. Carotenoids give the characteristic yellow color to autumn leaves, corn, canaries, daffodils, and lemons, as well as egg yolks, buttercups, and bananas. They absorb light energy and protect plants from photo damage in some cases. Sunlight has a slight yellowish hue when the Sun is near the horizon, due to atmospheric scattering of shorter wavelengths."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Cornwall_Daffodils.jpg/330px-Cornwall_Daffodils.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yellow","wikidata":"Q943"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Sillimanite-199671.jpg/500px-Sillimanite-199671.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Sillimanite-199671.jpg/500px-Sillimanite-199671.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Schist is a metamorphic rock characterized by an abundance of platy minerals. In this example, the rock has prominent sillimanite porphyroblasts as large as 3 cm (1.2 in).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASillimanite-199671.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Hubnerite-Quartz-216455.jpg/330px-Hubnerite-Quartz-216455.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Hubnerite-Quartz-216455.jpg/330px-Hubnerite-Quartz-216455.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hübnerite, the manganese-rich end-member of the wolframite series, with minor quartz in the background","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHubnerite-Quartz-216455.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Kaolinite-Orthoclase-lw18c.jpg/330px-Kaolinite-Orthoclase-lw18c.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Kaolinite-Orthoclase-lw18c.jpg/330px-Kaolinite-Orthoclase-lw18c.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"When minerals react, the products will sometimes assume the shape of the reagent; the product mineral is termed a pseudomorph of (or after) the reagent. Illustrated here is a pseudomorph of kaolinite after orthoclase. Here, the pseudomorph preserved the Carlsbad twinning common in orthoclase.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKaolinite-Orthoclase-lw18c.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Topaz-235220.jpg/500px-Topaz-235220.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Topaz-235220.jpg/500px-Topaz-235220.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Topaz has a characteristic orthorhombic elongated crystal shape.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATopaz-235220.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Spinel-4mb4c.jpg/330px-Spinel-4mb4c.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Spinel-4mb4c.jpg/330px-Spinel-4mb4c.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Contact twins, as seen in spinel","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASpinel-4mb4c.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Rough_diamond.jpg/330px-Rough_diamond.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia